Given a string s, find the first non-repeating character in it and return its index. If it does not exist, return -1.

 

Example 1:

Input: s = "leetcode"

Output: 0

Explanation:

The character 'l' at index 0 is the first character that does not occur at any other index.

Example 2:

Input: s = "loveleetcode"

Output: 2

Example 3:

Input: s = "aabb"

Output: -1


1. Simeple code Hashmap Freq one
class Solution {
    public int firstUniqChar(String s) {
        HashMap<Character,Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for(int i=0;i<s.length();i++){
            char ch = Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(i)); 
            map.put(ch,map.getOrDefault(ch,0)+1);
        }
        // Step 2: Loop through the string AGAIN to find the first unique character
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char ch = s.charAt(i);
            if (map.get(ch) == 1) {
                return i; // This 'i' represents the correct index in the string!
            }
        }
        // If no unique character is found, return -1
        return -1;
    }
}

2. save negative values when storing to finding dups again.
only unique will have index store against the ch

class Solution {
    public int firstUniqChar(String s) {
        Map<Character,Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for(int i=0;i<s.length();i++){
            char ch = s.charAt(i);
            if(map.containsKey(ch)){
               map.put(ch,-1);
            }
           else{
               map.put(ch,i);
           }
        }
            int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            for(char ch: map.keySet()){
                if(map.get(ch)>-1 && min>map.get(ch))
                {
                    min = map.get(ch);
                }
            }
        
        return min == Integer.MAX_VALUE?-1:min;
    }
}


The main difference between the two approaches is what the HashMap stores and how the second loop runs.
Here is a direct breakdown of how they compare:

Feature	Frequency Count Method (Two-Pass)	Index Tracking Method (The "Weird" Code)
What the Map Stores	Key: Character 
Value: Its total count (1, 2, 3...)	Key: Character 
Value: Its string index i (or -1 if duplicate)

Second Loop Targets	The entire original string from scratch.	Only the keys inside the map (max 26 letters).

Max Steps in Loop 2	Up to \(N\) steps (where \(N\) is the length of the string).	Max 26 steps (regardless of how long the string is).

Memory Cleanup	Keeps all character counts intact.	Destroys the original index numbers of duplicates by turning them into -1.
